  58→\n    59→            Note:\n    60→                See also :ref:`media` for more information regarding media\n    61→                handling.\n    62→\n    63→        text (str): String representing response content.\n    64→\n    65→            Note:\n    66→                Falcon will encode the given text as UTF-8\n    67→                in the response. If the content is already a byte string,\n    68→                use the :attr:`data` attribute instead (it's faster).\n    69→\n    70→        data (bytes): Byte string representing response content.\n    71→\n    72→            Use this attribute in lieu of `text` when your content is\n    73→            already a byte string (of type ``bytes``).\n    74→\n    75→            Warning:\n    76→                Always use the `text` attribute for text, or encode it\n    77→                first to ``bytes`` when using the `data` attribute, to\n    78→                ensure Unicode characters are properly encoded in the\n    79→                HTTP response.\n    80→\n    81→        stream: An async iterator or generator that yields a series of\n    82→            byte strings that will be streamed to the ASGI server as a\n    83→            series of \"http.response.body\" events. Falcon will assume the\n    84→            body is complete when the iterable is exhausted or as soon as it\n    85→            yields ``None`` rather than an instance of ``bytes``::\n    86→\n    87→                async def producer():\n    88→                    while True:\n    89→                        data_chunk = await read_data()\n    90→                        if not data_chunk:\n    91→                            break\n    92→\n    93→                        yield data_chunk\n    94→\n    95→                resp.stream = producer\n    96→\n    97→            Alternatively, a file-like object may be used as long as it\n    98→            implements an awaitable ``read()`` method::\n    99→\n   100→                resp.st