 the stream is known in advance. Using this\n   221\u2192        method is also slightly more performant as compared to setting the\n   222\u2192        properties individually.\n   223\u2192\n   224\u2192        Note:\n   225\u2192            If the stream length is unknown, you can set\n   226\u2192            :attr:`~falcon.asgi.Response.stream` directly, and ignore\n   227\u2192            :attr:`~falcon.asgi.Response.content_length`. In this case, the ASGI\n   228\u2192            server may choose to use chunked encoding for HTTP/1.1\n   229\u2192\n   230\u2192        Args:\n   231\u2192            stream: A readable, awaitable file-like object or async iterable\n   232\u2192                that returns byte strings. If the object implements a close()\n   233\u2192                method, it will be called after reading all of the data.\n   234\u2192            content_length (int): Length of the stream, used for the\n   235\u2192                Content-Length header in the response.\n   236\u2192        \"\"\"\n   237\u2192\n   238\u2192        self.stream = stream\n   239\u2192\n   240\u2192        # PERF(kgriffs): Set directly rather than incur the overhead of\n   241\u2192        #   the self.content_length property.\n   242\u2192        self._headers['content-length'] = str(content_length)\n   243\u2192\n   244\u2192    async def render_body(self):\n   245\u2192        \"\"\"Get the raw bytestring content for the response body.\n   246\u2192\n   247\u2192        This coroutine can be awaited to get the raw data for the\n   248\u2192        HTTP response body, taking into account the :attr:`~.text`,\n   249\u2192        :attr:`~.data`, and :attr:`~.media` attributes.\n   250\u2192\n   251\u2192        Note:\n   252\u2192            This method ignores :attr:`~.stream`; the caller must check\n   253\u2192            and handle that attribute directly.\n   254\u2192\n   255\u2192        Returns:\n   256\u2192            bytes: The UTF-8 encoded value of the `text` attribute, if\n   257\u2192            set. Otherwise, the value of the `data` attribute if set, or\n   258\u2192            finally the serialized value of the `media` attribute. If\n   259\u2192            none of these attributes are set, ``None`` is returned.\n   260\u2192        \"\"\"\n   261\u2192\n   262\u2192        # NOTE(vytas): The code below is also inlined in asgi.App.__call__.\n   263\u2192\n   264\u2192        text = self.text\n   265\u2192        if text is None:\n   266\u2192            data = self._data\n   267\u2192\n   268\u2192            if data is None and self._media is not None:\n   269\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): We use a special _UNSET singleton since\n   270\u2192                #   None is ambiguous (the media handler might return None).\n   271\u2192                if self._media_rendered is _UNSET:\n   272\u2192                    if not self.content_type:\n   273\u2192                        self.content_type = self.options.default_media_type\n   274\u2192\n   275\u2192                    handler, serialize_sync, _ = self.options.media_handlers._resolve(\n   276\u2192                        self.content_type, self.options.default_media_type\n   277\u2192                    )\n   278\u2192\n   279\u2192                    if serialize_sync:\n   280\u2192                        self._media_rendered = serialize_sync(self._media)\n   281\u2192                    else:\n   282\u2192                        self._media_rendered = await handler.serialize_async(\n   283\u2192                            self._media, self.content_type\n   284\u2192                        )\n   285\u2192\n   286\u2192                data = self._media_rendered\n   287\u2192        else:\n   288\u2192            try:\n   289\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): Normally we expect text to be a string\n   290\u2192                data = text.encode()\n   291\u2192            except AttributeError:\n   292\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): Assume it was a bytes object already\n   293\u2192                data = text\n   294\u2192\n   295\u2192        return data\n   296\u2192\n   297\u2192    def schedule(self, callback):\n   298\u2192        \"\"\"Schedule an async callback to run soon after sending the HTTP response.\n   299\u2192\n   300\u2192        This method can be used to execute a background job after the response\n   301\u2192        has been returned to the client.\n   302\u2192\n   303\u2192        The callback is assumed to be an async coroutine function. It will be\n   304\u2192        scheduled to run on the event loop as soon as possible.\n   305\u2192\n   306\u2192        The callback will be invoked without arguments. Use\n   307\u2192        :any:`functools.partial` to pass arguments to the callback as needed.\n   308\u2192\n   309\u2192        Note:\n   310\u2192            If an unhandled exception is raised while processing the request,\n   311\u2192            the callback will not be scheduled to run.\n   312\u2192\n   313\u2192        Note:\n   314\u2192            When an SSE emitter has been set on the response, the callback will\n   315\u2192            be scheduled before the first call to the emitter.\n   316\u2192\n   317\u2192        Warning:\n   318\u2192            Because coroutines run on the main request thread, care should\n   319\u2192            be taken to ensure they are non-blocking. Long-running operations\n   320\u2192            must use async libraries or delegate to an\n   321\u2192            :class:`~concurrent.futures.Executor` pool to avoid\n   322\u2192            blocking the processing of subsequent requests.\n   323\u2192\n   324\u2192        Args:\n   325\u2192            callback(object): An async coroutine function. The callback will be\n   326\u2192                invoked without arguments.\n   327\u2192        \"\"\"\n   328\u2192\n   329\u2192        if not iscoroutinefunction(callback):\n   330\u2192            if iscoroutine(callback):\n   331\u2192                raise TypeError(\n   332\u2192                    'The callback object appears to '\n   333\u2192                    'be a coroutine, rather than a coroutine function. Please '\n   334\u2192                    'pass the function itself, rather than the result obtained '\n   335\u2192                    'by calling the function. '\n   336\u2192                )\n   337\u2192            elif is_python_func(callback):  # pragma: nocover\n   338\u2192                raise TypeError('The callback must be a coroutine function.')\n   339\u2192\n   340\u2192            # NOTE(kgriffs): The implicit \"else\" branch is actually covered\n   341\u2192            #   by tests running in a Cython environment, but we can't\n   342\u2192            #   detect it with the coverage tool.\n   343\u2192\n   344\u2192        rc = (callback, True)\n   345\u2192\n   346\u2192        if not self._registered_callbacks:\n   347\u2192            self._registered_callbacks = [rc]\n   348\u2192        else:\n   349\u2192            self._registered_callbacks.append(rc)\n   350\u2192\n   351\u2192    def schedule_sync(self, callback):\n   352\u2192        \"\"\"Schedule a synchronous callback to run soon after sending the HTTP response.\n   353\u2192\n   354\u2192        This method can be used to execute a background job after the\n   355\u2192        response has been returned to the client.\n   356\u2192\n   357\u2192        The callback is assumed to be a synchronous (non-coroutine) function.\n   358\u2192        It will be scheduled on the event loop's default\n   359\u2192        :class:`~concurrent.futures.Executor` (which can be overridden via\n   360\u2192        :meth:`asyncio.AbstractEventLoop.set_default_executor`).\n   361\u2192\n   362\u2192        The callback will be invoked without arguments. Use\n   363\u2192        :any:`functools.partial` to pass arguments to the callback\n   364\u2192        as needed.\n   365\u2192\n   366\u2192        Note:\n   367\u2192            If an unhandled exception is raised while processing the request,\n   368\u2192            the callback will not be scheduled to run.\n   369\u2192\n   370\u2192        Note:\n   371\u2192            When an SSE emitter has been set on the response, the callback will\n   372\u2192            be scheduled before the first call to the emitter.\n   373\u2192\n   374\u2192        Warning:\n   375\u2192            Synchronous callables run on the event loop's default\n   376\u2192            :class:`~concurrent.futures.Executor`, which uses an instance of\n   377\u2192            :class:`~concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor` unless\n   378\u2192            :meth:`asyncio.AbstractEventLoop.set_default_executor` is used to\n   379\u2192            change it to something else. Due to the GIL, CPU-bound jobs will\n   380\u2192            block request processing for the current process unless the default\n   381\u2192            :class:`~concurrent.futures.Executor` is changed to one that is\n   382\u2192            process-based instead of thread-based (e.g., an instance of\n   383\u2192            :class:`concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor`).\n   384\u2192\n   385\u2192        Args:\n   386\u2192            callback(object): An async coroutine function or a synchronous\n   387\u2192                callable. The callback will be called without arguments.\n   388\u2192        \"\"\"\n   389\u2192\n   390\u2192        rc = (callback, False)\n   391\u2192\n   392\u2192        if not self._registered_callbacks:\n   393\u2192            self._registered_callbacks = [rc]\n   394\u2192        else:\n   395\u2192            self._registered_callbacks.append(rc)\n   396\u2192\n   397\u2192    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   398\u2192    # Helper methods\n   399\u2192    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   400\u2192\n   401\u2192    def _asgi_headers(self, media_type=None):\n   402\u2192        \"\"\"Convert headers into the format expected by ASGI servers.\n   403\u2192\n   404\u2192        Header names must be lowercased and both name and value must be\n   405\u2192        byte strings.\n   406\u2192\n   407\u2192        See also: https://asgi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/specs/www.html#response-start\n   408\u2192\n   409\u2192        Args:\n   410\u2192            media_type: Default media type to use for the Content-Type\n   411\u2192                header if the header was not set explicitly (default ``None``).\n   412\u2192\n   413\u2192        \"\"\"\n   414\u2192\n   415\u2192        headers = self._headers\n   416\u2192        # PERF(vytas): uglier inline version of Response._set_media_type\n   417\u2192        if media_type is not None and 'content-type' not in headers:\n   418\u2192            headers['content-type'] = media_type\n   419\u2192\n   420\u2192        try:\n   421\u2192            # NOTE(vytas): Supporting ISO-8859-1 for historical reasons as per\n   422\u2192            #   RFC 7230, Section 3.2.4; and to strive for maximum\n   423\u2192            #   compatibility with WSGI.\n   424\u2192\n   425\u2192            # PERF(vytas): On CPython, _encode_items_to_latin1 is implemented\n   426\u2192            #   in Cython (with a pure Python fallback), where the resulting\n   427\u2192            #   C code speeds up the method substantially by directly invoking\n   428\u2192            #   CPython's C API functions such as PyUnicode_EncodeLatin1.\n   429\u2192            items = _encode_items_to_latin1(headers)\n   430\u2192        except UnicodeEncodeError as ex:\n   431\u2192            # TODO(vytas): In 3.1.0, update this error message to highlight the\n   432\u2192            #   fact that we decided to allow ISO-8859-1?\n   433\u2192            raise ValueError(\n   434\u2192                'The modern series of HTTP standards require that header '\n   435\u2192                f'names and values use only ASCII characters: {ex}'\n   436\u2192            )\n   437\u2192\n   438\u2192        if self._extra_headers:\n   439\u2192            items += [\n   440\u2192                (n.encode('ascii'), v.encode('ascii')) for n, v in self._extra_headers\n   441\u2192            ]\n   442\u2192\n   443\u2192        # NOTE(kgriffs): It is important to append these after self._extra_headers\n   444\u2192        #   in case the latter contains Set-Cookie headers that should be\n   445\u2192        #   overridden by a call to unset_cookie().\n   446\u2192        if self._cookies is not None:\n   447\u2192            # PERF(tbug):\n   448\u2192            # The below implementation is ~23% faster than\n   449\u2192            # the alternative:\n   450\u2192            #\n   451\u2192            #     self._cookies.output().split(\"\\\\r\\\\n\")\n   452\u2192            #\n   453\u2192            # Even without the .split(\"\\\\r\\\\n\"), the below\n   454\u2192            # is still ~17% faster, so don't use .output()\n   455\u2192            items += [\n   456\u2192                (b'set-cookie', c.OutputString().encode('ascii'))\n   457\u2192                for c in self._cookies.values()\n   458\u2192            ]\n   459\u2192        return items\n   460\u2192\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
